Yices macOS relink kit
======================

You do not need this kit to install or run Yices
-------------------------------------------------

Use the normal Yices distribution archive to install Yices. This separate kit
contains the Yices object code, the exact dependency archives, the link
commands needed to relink the four command-line tools and the dynamic library
after modifying GMP or LibPoly, and the license texts and notices for the
components it redistributes.

The kit is self-contained. It does not need a Yices source or build tree.


Contents
--------

  README                         this file
  BUILDINFO                      production identity and five link commands
  MANIFEST.sha256                checksum for every other shipped file
  LICENSE                        Yices' own license terms
  NOTICES                        the components in this kit and their notices
  executables/                   four front-end object files
  deps/                          Yices and dependency archives
  licenses/                      license texts that accompany this kit
  relink-executables.sh          rebuilds the four tools
  relink-library.sh              rebuilds libyices.2.dylib
  verify-manifest.sh             verifies the extracted kit

The normal and PIC LibPoly archives have the same byte count on the official
builders but different contents. The scripts select them by path. The three
full front ends use deps/libpoly.a. The dynamic library uses
deps/libpicpoly.a. yices-sat uses neither one and also uses no GMP archive.

The GMP inventory is derived from the configured release inputs. An Apple
Silicon kit contains deps/libgmp-static.a for the executables and a distinct
deps/libgmp-pic.a for the dynamic library. The Intel release uses one fat PIC
archive for both payloads, so its kit contains only deps/libgmp-static.a and
the library script selects that same file. BUILDINFO and MANIFEST.sha256 record
which arrangement the kit contains.

All dependency archives are named explicitly and occur in the same order as in
the production links. The scripts do not use -L or -l for those archives, so
they cannot select a library from the machine running the relink.


Requirements
------------

Run the scripts on macOS with command-line developer tools for the same native
architecture as the kit. They require cc, codesign, lipo, otool, and either
shasum or sha256sum. The scripts export MACOSX_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET=11.0 and link
the C++ runtime as -lc++.

Relinking with a different compiler or SDK from the production versions in
BUILDINFO may change the output or fail. The kit promises relinkability, not a
byte-identical result.


Verify, relink, and inspect
---------------------------

From the extracted kit root:

    ./verify-manifest.sh
    ./relink-executables.sh
    ./relink-library.sh

The output defaults to ./relinked. Each script prints its full link command.
The outputs are ad hoc signed because modern macOS requires a valid signature
after a link changes Mach-O contents.

Inspect the four executables:

    for f in relinked/yices relinked/yices-sat \
             relinked/yices-smt relinked/yices-smt2; do
        "$f" --version
        lipo -info "$f"
        otool -L "$f"
        otool -l "$f" | grep -A3 LC_BUILD_VERSION
    done

Inspect the dynamic library:

    otool -D relinked/libyices.2.dylib
    otool -L relinked/libyices.2.dylib
    lipo -info relinked/libyices.2.dylib
    otool -l relinked/libyices.2.dylib | grep -A3 LC_BUILD_VERSION

The install name must be exactly @rpath/libyices.2.dylib. The only load
dependencies must be /usr/lib/libSystem.B.dylib and
/usr/lib/libc++.1.dylib. Every LC_BUILD_VERSION must report minos 11.0.


Replace a library
-----------------

Either replace the corresponding file under deps/ or provide an environment
override. For example:

    GMP_A=/path/to/libgmp.a SKIP_VERIFY=1 ./relink-executables.sh
    LIBPOLY_A=/path/to/libpicpoly.a SKIP_VERIFY=1 ./relink-library.sh

Set SKIP_VERIFY=1 after intentionally changing an input, because the original
manifest should and will reject changed bytes. The library-side GMP and
LibPoly inputs must be position-independent.

These are the inputs relink-executables.sh accepts, besides the test hook
described under "Archive order guard" below.

    CC             compiler driver, default cc
    CFLAGS         compile and link flags, see below
    LIBYICES_A     default deps/libyices.a
    GMP_A          default deps/libgmp-static.a
    LIBPOLY_A      default deps/libpoly.a
    CUDD_A         default deps/libcudd.a
    CADICAL_A      default deps/libcadical.a
    CMS_A          default deps/libcryptominisat5.a
    KISSAT_A       default deps/libkissat_yices.a
    SKIP_VERIFY    set to 1 to bypass the manifest check

The first argument is the output directory. It defaults to ./relinked.

relink-library.sh accepts the same set with three differences. Its LIBPOLY_A
defaults to deps/libpicpoly.a. Its GMP_A defaults to deps/libgmp-pic.a when
the kit contains that file and to deps/libgmp-static.a otherwise, which is the
arrangement described above. It adds LIBNAME, default libyices.2.dylib, and
INSTALL_NAME, default @rpath/libyices.2.dylib.

There is no input for a C library, and there is none in deps/. macOS supplies
the C library as part of the operating system, so no C library is built into
these binaries and none is replaced here.

BUILDINFO records, for each output, a link command assembled from the same
variables the production rules used. It is not a capture of the command line
the linker received. Treat the corresponding link-* entry as the reference
command. The command a script prints is not identical to it. These are the
differences.

  CC defaults to cc, while BUILDINFO records gcc as the driver. Both resolve
  to the same Apple toolchain compiler on a normal macOS installation.

  CFLAGS defaults to
  -fvisibility=hidden -O3 -fomit-frame-pointer -fno-stack-protector.
  BUILDINFO records those same four flags with -Wall and -Wredundant-decls
  inserted after -fvisibility=hidden. Those two control diagnostics and do not
  change the generated code.

  Every link-* entry in BUILDINFO begins with MACOSX_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET=11.0.
  The scripts export that variable instead of printing it, so it does not
  appear in the printed command.

  The scripts resolve the kit to an absolute path and print an absolute
  pathname for every object file and archive. BUILDINFO records the same files
  as kit-relative paths, such as executables/yices.o and deps/libcudd.a.

  The scripts write into the output directory, so the printed command ends
  with an output path such as -o ./relinked/yices. BUILDINFO records -o yices.

Setting CC and CFLAGS to the values BUILDINFO records closes the first two
differences. The last three remain. The deployment target is already exported
with the value BUILDINFO records, and the two path differences follow from
where you unpacked the kit and where you sent the output. Compare the two
commands input by input rather than as strings.


Archive order guard
-------------------

GMP must remain the final archive in the full executable and dynamic-library
lists. Both scripts enforce that ordering before checking files or invoking the
compiler. The test hook below deliberately moves a non-archive argument after
GMP and must fail with exit status 1:

    RELINK_TEST_GMP_NOT_LAST=1 SKIP_VERIFY=1 ./relink-executables.sh
    RELINK_TEST_GMP_NOT_LAST=1 SKIP_VERIFY=1 ./relink-library.sh

This hook changes no normal invocation. It exists so the guard itself can be
tested rather than merely inspected.


License texts and notices
-------------------------

GMP and LibPoly are distributed in this release under the GNU Lesser General
Public License version 3 or later. That license incorporates the GNU General
Public License version 3 by reference, so licenses/ carries both full texts.

There is no glibc archive in a macOS Yices binary. The macOS kits therefore do
not carry LGPL version 2.1 terms.

The BSD, MIT and Apache License 2.0 texts are present because components in
deps/ are distributed under them, and NOTICES records which component each
applies to. licenses/ is not a complete set of the terms that apply to those
components. Where a component's terms are not in licenses/, NOTICES reproduces
them instead, as it does for CUDD.

CUDD, CaDiCaL, Kissat, and CryptoMiniSat are here because their archives are
necessary for relinking. This kit publishes no relinking material for them.
Their notices are here because this kit redistributes those archives.

NOTICES, in this kit, records each third-party component the kit redistributes, where it
came from, and the terms its own authors distribute it under.
